artners so while we can't say word one  about the quality and effectiveness of  rage 2's new structure or how Avalanche  it really is its telltale gunplay is  immediately recognizable the question  now however is how the added layers on  top factor in is your character develops  new abilities and acquires new weapons  over time  replaying the original rage prior to the  event reminded me how much I enjoyed its  combat in level design the smartly  orchestrated battles in clever AI made  of her engaging fights where using cover  and flanking was key quite the opposite  Forge to encourages you to dive headlong  into a fire fight like 2016's doom you  stand to benefit from fighting face to  face and a quick dash maneuver has been  introduced to help you get in and out of  fights quickly rather than lavishly  dismembering your enemies to earn ammo  and health running into a fray after  killing a few enemies from afar allows  you to pick up energy reserves that  recharge super-powered attacks such as a  ground pound and a force push you've  also got access to an overdrive meter  that can be activated to run faster and  hit harder transforming an imposing  group of enemies into unfortunate  sitting ducks much the same way that you  power up and lay waste to smaller  enemies and destiny - to be blunt twenty  minutes to trips through the same demo  isn't nearly enough time to form a  confident opinion about rage choose  numerous combat mechanics it's fair  however to say that you'll be encouraged  to be both proactive and creative to get  the most out of the game's expanded  potential the not quite a boomerang wing  stick is back and like its advanced form  and rage you can have it home in on  multiple enemies before you let it fly  or charge it up to spin in place once it  lands on a single target for prolonged  damage output  but those just footage from its e3  showcase featured a creative combo  attack with a grenade in a wing stick to  send a disabled enemy flying upward